    "https://www.dwu.edu/office-of-disability-services": {
      "title": "DWU",
      "description": "DWU | Office of Disability Services Skip to main content Search the site Search Athletics Performing Arts Menu Close Academics Undergraduate Programs Graduate & Online A.S. in Nursing (Rapid City Campus) A.S. in Nursing (Sioux Falls Campus) Degree Completion Mitchell Scholars Program Summer Courses Internships Academic Calendar Academic Catalogs Admissions For Parents & Families Scholarships & Financial Aid Tuition & Fees Visit Campus New Student Registration The First Year Experience International Students Transfer Students Request Information Meet Our Admissions Staff Student Life Academic & Career Services Center Graduate Studies Student Handbook Office of Disability Services Student Handbook Student Life Staff Student Services Title IX TRIO Student Support Services Alumni & Giving Alumni & Giving Giving Alumni President’s Circle Blue & White Days Wesleyan Today Alumni Magazine Advancement Staff About DWU Why DWU Why DWU DWU History Mission & Values Accreditation DWU Facts News Events Who We Are Board of Trustees President’s Office and Cabinet Leadership Council Faculty & Staff Directory Campus Map About Mitchell Consumer Information FAQs Our Services DWU Store McGovern Library For Employers Employment at DWU Hold Your Events at DWU McGovern Center for Leadership and Public Service The Kelley Center for Entrepreneurship Visit Give Apply Undergraduate Programs Explore more than 40 majors and minors. Graduate & Online Explore on-campus and online graduate programs. A.S. in Nursing (Rapid City Campus) A.S. in Nursing (Sioux Falls Campus) Degree Completion Mitchell Scholars Program Summer Courses Internships Academic Calendar Academic Catalogs Please Select... A.S. in Nursing (Rapid City Campus) A.S. in Nursing (Sioux Falls Campus) Degree Completion Mitchell Scholars Program Summer Courses Internships Academic Calendar Academic Catalogs Apply for Free Begin an amazing new chapter in your life. Admission Requirements You belong at DWU. Take a look at the requirements for admission. For Parents & Families Scholarships & Financial Aid Tuition & Fees Visit Campus New Student Registration The First Year Experience International Students Transfer Students Request Information Meet Our Admissions Staff Please Select... For Parents & Families Scholarships & Financial Aid Tuition & Fees Visit Campus New Student Registration The First Year Experience International Students Transfer Students Request Information Meet Our Admissions Staff Life at DWU Living on campus is a great opportunity to make lifelong friends. Student Activities and Organizations We offer a variety of ways to get involved on campus. Academic & Career Services Center Graduate Studies Student Handbook Office of Disability Services Student Handbook Student Life Staff Student Services Title IX TRIO Student Support Services Please Select... Academic & Career Services Center Graduate Studies Student Handbook Office of Disability Services Student Handbook Student Life Staff Student Services Title IX TRIO Student Support Services Giving Every gift helps our students, no matter how large or small. Alumni There are many ways to stay connected to Dakota Wesleyan University. Alumni & Giving President’s Circle Blue & White Days Wesleyan Today Alumni Magazine Advancement Staff Please Select... Alumni & Giving President’s Circle Blue & White Days Wesleyan Today Alumni Magazine Advancement Staff Why DWU Why DWU DWU History Mission & Values Accreditation DWU Facts News Events Who We Are Board of Trustees President’s Office and Cabinet Leadership Council Faculty & Staff Directory Campus Map About Mitchell Consumer Information FAQs Our Services DWU Store McGovern Library For Employers Employment at DWU Hold Your Events at DWU McGovern Center for Leadership and Public Service The Kelley Center for Entrepreneurship Why DWU Why DWU DWU History Mission & Values Accreditation DWU Facts News Events Who We Are Board of Trustees President’s Office and Cabinet Leadership Council Faculty & Staff Directory Campus Map About Mitchell Consumer Information FAQs Our Services DWU Store McGovern Library For Employers Employment at DWU Hold Your Events at DWU McGovern Center for Leadership and Public Service The Kelley Center for Entrepreneurship Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Menu Close Academics Undergraduate Programs Graduate & Online A.S. in Nursing (Rapid City Campus) A.S. in Nursing (Sioux Falls Campus) Degree Completion Mitchell Scholars Program Summer Courses Internships Academic Calendar Academic Catalogs Admissions For Parents & Families Scholarships & Financial Aid Tuition & Fees Visit Campus New Student Registration The First Year Experience International Students Transfer Students Request Information Meet Our Admissions Staff Student Life Academic & Career Services Center Graduate Studies Student Handbook Office of Disability Services Student Handbook Student Life Staff Student Services Title IX TRIO Student Support Services Alumni & Giving Alumni & Giving Giving Alumni President’s Circle Blue & White Days Wesleyan Today Alumni Magazine Advancement Staff About DWU Why DWU Why DWU DWU History Mission & Values Accreditation DWU Facts News Events Who We Are Board of Trustees President’s Office and Cabinet Leadership Council Faculty & Staff Directory Campus Map About Mitchell Consumer Information FAQs Our Services DWU Store McGovern Library For Employers Employment at DWU Hold Your Events at DWU McGovern Center for Leadership and Public Service The Kelley Center for Entrepreneurship Athletics Performing Arts Visit Give Apply Now instagram facebook twitter linkedin About DWU Office of Disability Services This office ensures that students with disabilities have equal access and full participation in\n        postsecondary education as outlined by Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973. What We Do Coordinate academic support services and accommodations . Advocate for students with disabilities. Promote student development and self-advocacy. Serve as a campus resource on disability awareness and accommodations. Ensure that student information remains confidential. How do I Request Services? Contact the DWU Disability Services Coordinator to assist with the completion of the following steps: Self-identify as having a disability. Complete the Disability Services Registration form or the Short-Term Disability Registration form. Complete the Disabilities Services Release form. Complete the Request for Accommodations form. Submit letter(s) of accommodation. Accommodations At DWU we understand that everyone learns differently, and we want to give everyone the opportunity to learn. This might mean a reasonable accommodation is in\n        order. Common accommodations are, but are not limited to: Alternative Testing Areas Extended Testing Time If your academic success depends on a reasonable set of accommodations, we will work with you to find and implement a strategic\n        solution. What is considered a disability? We use the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) as a guide to define disability: a condition that substantially limits a major life activity, such as walking,\n        talking, learning, hearing and/or seeing. Services will be provided to students with physical, psychiatric, and/or learning disabilities, chronic health conditions, mobility, visual, speech\n        and coordination impairments. If you have a temporary condition such as a broken arm, sprained ankle, etc., you are also eligible for short-term services as needed. What type of documentation is required? Documentation of a disability is necessary to initiate and receive services through the Office of Disability Services. If you don’t have the proper\n        documentation to support your requests for accommodation, you will need to get the documentation (test, diagnosis, etc.) at your own expense. High school juniors and seniors should know that elementary and high schools are required to retest students for disabilities every three years, and that when a\n        student nears graduation, the school is required to develop a transition plan to help prepare the student for life after high school. If this applies to you, you should take advantage of this transition plan and get new testing if necessary. Other students may need a letter from a physician,\n        psychologist or psychiatrist to document a specific physical or mental limitation. Confidentiality The Office of Disability Services is committed to ensuring that all information regarding a student is maintained as confidential as required by\n        law. Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 “No otherwise qualified handicapped individual in the United States shall, solely by reason of handicap, be excluded from the participation in, be denied the\n        benefits of or be subjected to discrimination under any program or activity receiving federal financial assistance.” Questions? Please contact the Disability Services Coordinator for questions and assistance. Kelli Mueller, M.Ed. Disability Services Coordinator | Student Success Adviser Phone: (605) 995-2155 Email: Kelli.Mueller@dwu.edu Quick Links Student Central Wesleyan Central Campus Map McGovern Library Academic Catalog Academic Calendar About Us COVID-19 Information Privacy Policy 1200 W. University Ave. Mitchell, SD 57301 605-995-2600 Contact Us instagram facebook twitter linkedin flickr youtube © 2026 Dakota Wesleyan University Website By morweb.org Student Central Wesleyan Central Campus Map McGovern Library Academic Catalog Academic Calendar About Us COVID-19 Information Privacy Policy Check Links Check SEO × SEO Report Placeholder issue 1: This is a test issue. Placeholder issue 2: Another test issue. Check Performance × Performance Report Placeholder issue 1: This is a test issue. Placeholder issue 2: Another test issue.