nts for admission. For Parents & Families Scholarships & Financial Aid Tuition & Fees Visit Campus New Student Registration The First Year Experience International Students Transfer Students Request Information Meet Our Admissions Staff Please Select... For Parents & Families Scholarships & Financial Aid Tuition & Fees Visit Campus New Student Registration The First Year Experience International Students Transfer Students Request Information Meet Our Admissions Staff Life at DWU Living on campus is a great opportunity to make lifelong friends. Student Activities and Organizations We offer a variety of ways to get involved on campus. Academic & Career Services Center Graduate Studies Student Handbook Office of Disability Services Student Handbook Student Life Staff Student Services Title IX TRIO Student Support Services Please Select... Academic & Career Services Center Graduate Studies Student Handbook Office of Disability Services Student Handbook Student Life Staff Student Services Title IX TRIO Student Support Services Giving Every gift helps our students, no matter how large or small. Alumni There are many ways to stay connected to Dakota Wesleyan University. Alumni & Giving President’s Circle Blue & White Days Wesleyan Today Alumni Magazine Advancement Staff Please Select... Alumni & Giving President’s Circle Blue & White Days Wesleyan Today Alumni Magazine Advancement Staff Why DWU Why DWU DWU History Mission & Values Accreditation DWU Facts News Events Who We Are Board of Trustees President’s Office and Cabinet Leadership Council Faculty & Staff Directory Campus Map About Mitchell Consumer Information FAQs Our Services DWU Store McGovern Library For Employers Employment at DWU Hold Your Events at DWU McGovern Center for Leadership and Public Service The Kelley Center for Entrepreneurship Why DWU Why DWU DWU History Mission & Values Accreditation DWU Facts News Events Who We Are Board of Trustees President’s Office and Cabinet Leadership Council Faculty & Staff Directory Campus Map About Mitchell Consumer Information FAQs Our Services DWU Store McGovern Library For Employers Employment at DWU Hold Your Events at DWU McGovern Center for Leadership and Public Service The Kelley Center for Entrepreneurship Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Please Select... Menu Close Academics Undergraduate Programs Graduate & Online A.S. in Nursing (Rapid City Campus) A.S. in Nursing (Sioux Falls Campus) Degree Completion Mitchell Scholars Program Summer Courses Internships Academic Calendar Academic Catalogs Admissions For Parents & Families Scholarships & Financial Aid Tuition & Fees Visit Campus New Student Registration The First Year Experience International Students Transfer Students Request Information Meet Our Admissions Staff Student Life Academic & Career Services Center Graduate Studies Student Handbook Office of Disability Services Student Handbook Student Life Staff Student Services Title IX TRIO Student Support Services Alumni & Giving Alumni & Giving Giving Alumni President’s Circle Blue & White Days Wesleyan Today Alumni Magazine Advancement Staff About DWU Why DWU Why DWU DWU History Mission & Values Accreditation DWU Facts News Events Who We Are Board of Trustees President’s Office and Cabinet Leadership Council Faculty & Staff Directory Campus Map About Mitchell Consumer Information FAQs Our Services DWU Store McGovern Library For Employers Employment at DWU Hold Your Events at DWU McGovern Center for Leadership and Public Service The Kelley Center for Entrepreneurship Athletics Performing Arts Visit Give Apply Now instagram facebook twitter linkedin Living at DWU Residential LIfe Getting ready to move to college should be the most exciting time in your life right now! We want you to be fully prepared, so here’s everything you need to know ahead of time. Living at DWU We can't wait to have you on campus! To prepare for your arrival, please complete the Housing Intention Form. Yes, all students, even students not\n        planning to live on campus, must complete this form! Housing Intention Form Move-In Dates New Student Orientation Schedule Room Layouts + What to Bring DWU Parking & Dining Moving into your new residence hall can be a tiring experience for you and your family, which is why we do our best to keep it as fun and stress-free as possible!\n        If you are moving into Dayton Hall we’ll have staff, faculty and current students available to help you carry things to your room. The Big Move: August 21, 2026 New Student Orientation (NSO): August 21-23 Classes Begin – August 24, 2026 Fall 2026 sport-specific move-in dates: ~ July 7 – Freshman Football ~ July 31 – Returning Football, Volleyball, Women’s Soccer, Men’s Soccer ~ Aug. 10 – Cross Country, Women’s Golf, Men’s Golf ~ Aug. 17 – Cheer Friday, August 21 The Big Move – Move-In & Welcome Events 8 a.m. - 12 p.m. – Move-In 8 a.m. - 9:15 a.m. | Students with Dayton Hall room numbers ending in 1, 2, 3 9:15 a.m. - 10:30 a.m. | Students with Dayton Hall room numbers ending in 4, 5, 6, 7 10:30 a.m. - 12 p.m. | Students with Dayton Hall room numbers ending in 8, 9, 0 plus ALL rooms in Koka Hall Note: A mobile coffee trailer will be on campus during move-in. 12 p.m. - 1:30 p.m. – Family Picnic/BBQ & Errand Break Families can enjoy a meal together. Optional time for families to shop for essentials. 1 p.m. - 3:30 p.m. – Finalization Tasks - students will receive a checklist of tasks to complete at move-in (various locations in the Sherman\n                Center/Rollins Campus Center) Financial Aid/Business Office - Lower Level Rollins Campus Nurse - Lower Level Rollins College ID Picture - Lower Level Rollins Get iPad - Sherman Center 1 p.m. - 1:30 p.m. (Freshmen, last name begins with A-G) 1:30 p.m. - 2 p.m. (Freshmen, last name begins with H-O) 2 p.m. - 2:30 p.m. (Freshmen, last name begins with P-Z) 2:30 p.m. - 3:30 p.m. (Transfer students) 4 p.m. - 4:30 p.m. – Welcome Gathering in Sherman Center 4:30 p.m. - 5 p.m. – Family Goodbyes & Departure 5:30 p.m. - 6:30 p.m. – Tailgate 6:30 p.m. - 8 p.m. – Football Scrimmage Yard games, music, and social time Ice Cream Social with the President Kittle's Family Saturday, August 22 Orientation & Campus Exploration 7 a.m. - 9:30 a.m. – Breakfast 10 a.m. - 11:30 a.m. – Tools for Success on the First Day of Class 11:30 a.m. - 12:30 p.m. – Lunch 12:30 p.m. - 4:30 p.m. – Rotating Orientation Sessions (with Uni101 Groups) Students will rotate through the following sessions: Session A – Scavenger Hunt Explore key campus locations and take pictures of your group at each space. Session B – Campus FAQ Frequently asked questions about DWU. Session C – College Success Strategies Learn time management, study habits, and campus resources. Session D – Getting Involved at DWU Overview of student organizations, intramurals, and campus events. 5:30 p.m. - 6:30 p.m. – Dinner 7 p.m. - 9 p.m. – Evening Social Activities Yard games on Dayton Lawn DWU Theater performance Sunday, August 23 Community Engagement & Preparation for Classes 7 a.m. - 9:30 a.m. – Athletic Practices 10 a.m. - 12 p.m. – Attend Fusion Church Service (or other services in Mitchell) 11:30 a.m. - 12:30 p.m. – Lunch 12:30 p.m. - 3 p.m. – Food Drive Service Project (with Uni101 Groups) 5:30 p.m. - 6:30 p.m. – Dinner 7 p.m. - 8:30 p.m. – Final Preparation for First Day of Classes Floor meetings with RAs Academic tips & Q&A If you are having trouble making one of our move-in dates work, please contact Student\n        Life. Grid List Heather Miller-Cink Dean of Students Heather brings 35 years of experience in education, including 14 years as a choral music educator and 21 years in high school administration. She spent nearly three decades of her career in Osseo Area Schools, a diverse district just north of Minneapolis. Her work has centered on collaborative leadership, fostering a positive school culture, and promoting equitable student achievement. Abby Ferris Residence Life Lead Heather Miller-Cink Dean of Students Heather brings 35 years of experience in education, including 14 years as a choral music educator and 21 years in high school administration. She spent nearly three decades of her career in Osseo Area Schools, a diverse district just north of Minneapolis. Her work has centered on collaborative leadership, fostering a positive school culture, and promoting equitable student achievement. Abby Ferris Residence Life Lead Show More Room Layouts As a freshman, you will live in Dayton Hall. This is a great opportunity to build new friendships with other freshman students. Rooms on the north side of Dayton Hall are approximately 15'×11' , while rooms on the south side are approximately 12'×12' . View North Side Room Layout Room numbers are 3 digits (example: 103) View South Side Room Layout Room numbers are 4 digits (example: 1103) There are common bathrooms on each floor and a common lounge. Each room has: In-room A/C and heat control Free wi-fi Two twin extra-long beds with mattresses Two closets Vanity area with mirror and drawers Lofts must be ordered and paid for before move-in. After your freshman year, you will have options to live in our Allen Hall pods and suites, Koka Hall, Dakota Hall, campus-owned houses or in an off-campus\n        location. Explore campus virtually College Dorm Packing Checklist (printable PDF) What to Bring ....Not the kitchen sink! Packing for college is an exciting time, and we want to make sure\n        you’re able to hit the ground running when you arrive at DWU with everything you’ll need to feel at home. We’ve put together a catch-all list of items most students tend to find useful. However, please note that this list is not comprehensive, and it may help you to visit campus to get a\n        look at a residence hall room before you begin buying supplies. School supplies such as pens, highlighters, notebooks, Post-It notes and calendar/planner Items for your Twin XL bed: pillow, bedding, mattress pad Clothes hangers Coffeemaker Dishes, cups and silverware Poster putty or 3M tabs for hanging up decorations Headphones or earbuds Cell phone and other chargers Power strip with surge protection Futon, small couch or other seating (optional) Desk Lamp TV (there is a common area TV if you don’t bring one) Small fan Air freshener Mini fridge and/or microwave (you can discuss this with your roommate) Small shelving unit Cleaning Supplies Message board for your door What to Wear Comfortable clothes and shoes (most students wear sweats or jeans to class) A few dressy outfits and shoes for more formal affairs (such as banquets) Workout clothes and shoes Heavy coat, boots, mittens, scarf, hat (it gets cold in Mitchell, and you will want to be prepared) Slippers For Your Room We’ve put together a catch-all list of items most students tend to find useful. However,\n        please note that this list is not comprehensive, and it may help you to visit campus to get a look at a residence hall room before you begin buying supplies. Area Rug or Carpet Remnant (Dayton Hall has tile floors) Rolls of quarters for laundry Items for your Twin XL bed: pillow, bedding, mattress pad Clothes hangers Coffeemaker (We do have Starbucks on campus!) Dishes, cups and silverware Poster putty or 3M tabs for hanging up decorations Small shelving unit Trash can Power strip with surge protection Futon, small couch or other seating Desk Lamp Small Fan TV (there is a common area TV if you don’t bring one) Air freshener Mini fridge and/or microwave (you can discuss this with your roommate) Cleaning Supplies Message board for your door Personal Items Keep in mind that you’ll be sharing your bathroom and laundry facilities with other\n        students! Bathrobe and Shower Shoes Towels and washcloths Shampoo, conditioner, body wash, shaving cream, and razors Shower caddy Laundry basket or bag Laundry detergent and dryer sheets Feminine hygiene products First-aid kit Personal medications Other Things Think about your daily activities and hobbies and bring what you need with you. Access to money (i.e., bank account, bank card, credit card, etc.); your bank may or may not have a presence in Mitchell. Renter's insurance (have your parents check their homeowner’s insurance to see if you are covered under their policy) Key ring or lanyard Flashlight and b atteries Headphones or earbuds Laptop optional (You will be receiving an iPad.) Non-perishable snacks for your room Do Not Bring T he following items are prohibited: Halogen bulbs or lamps Toasters, toaster ovens, hot plates, skillets or air fryers Air conditioners (rooms have AC) Candles Pets Decor that requires nails Weapons of any kind Juuls or vape pens (we are a smoke free campus) May I live off campus? DWU encourages all students to live on campus. Here’s why: Better academics: According to the American Council on Education, students who live on campus\n                earn higher GPAs, are more likely to graduate and are more satisfied with their college experience. Lasting friendships: Students who live on campus make friends easier and form stronger\n                friendships. Students who live off campus often miss out on opportunities to bond, and report feeling disconnected and less engaged than their on-campus\n                peers. Convenient and affordable: To get to class, you just roll out of bed and you’re halfway\n                there. With on-campus dining, laundry and other resources, you won’t have to worry about grocery shopping, washing dishes, paying for utilities or cable, furniture, monthly rent\n                payments or gas money for commutes. Safe and secure: Our campus is safe, and the residence halls are secure and staffed 24 hours a\n                day. Participation: Students who live on campus are more likely to participate in activities, groups,\n                ministry and leadership opportunities. When students live off campus, they’re much less likely to get involved and have a harder time making friends. Support: Students who live on campus benefit from the support of community with their peers as\n                well as the emotional, academic and spiritual support of their resident adviser. You may live off campus if : You are 21 years of age or older by the first day of the semester. You have been disciplinarily dismissed or banned from the residence halls. You will be living with parent(s)/guardian(s) within 40 miles of campus. You are enrolled as a part-time student, taking less than 12 credits. You are married. You have dependent children living with you. You can read more about the housing policy in the student\n        handbook . DWU Dining Dining with friends and peers is one of the best college experiences. Our meal plans allow for flexibility that fits in perfectly with your lifestyle. Enjoy an\n        all-you-care-to-eat buffet at D-Dub's with one swipe of your I.D. A huge selection of meal plans is available, and we are excited to offer vegetarian, gluten-free and other dining options to accommodate your dietary\n        needs. Tiger Café Sometimes you need a boost of caffeine while you study, write or just hang out. That’s why we built a Starbucks coffee shop right into our library. Enjoy a\n        smoothie, refresher, pink drink, cold brew or blond blend at the Tiger Café in the McGovern Library. And best of all, your meal card works here, too! DWU Parking DWU offers a variety of parking locations across campus. Best of all – parking is FREE! Take a moment to register your vehicle for your free parking pass by\n        following the steps below: Log in to TigerNet Click “Student Life” Click “My Vehicle Information” Click “Add Vehicle” and fill out the information. Questions Connect with DWU Student Life Email: studentlife@dwu.edu Phone: 605-995-2944 Quick Links Student Central Wesleyan Central Campus Map McGovern Library Academic Catalog Academic Calendar About Us COVID-19 Information Privacy Policy 1200 W. University Ave. Mitchell, SD 57301 605-995-2600 Contact Us instagram facebook twitter linkedin flickr youtube © 2026 Dakota Wesleyan University Website By morweb.org Student Central Wesleyan Central Campus Map McGovern Library Academic Catalog Academic Calendar About Us COVID-19 Information Privacy Policy Check Links Check SEO × SEO Report Placeholder issue 1: This is a test issue. Placeholder issue 2: Another test issue. Check Performance × Performance Report Placeholder issue 1: This is a test issue. Placeholder issue 2: Another test issue. Check Accessibility Back Department: Location: Phone: Campus Mail Box: Year Started at DWU: Education History: Professional History: Strengths: Area of Expertise: Hobbies: Family: What I Love Most About DWU: Quick Links Menu Student Central Wesleyan Central Campus Map McGovern Library Academic Catalog Academic Calendar About Us COVID-19 Information Privacy Policy Accessibility High Contrast Dark White text on black backgrounds (WCAG-style) High Contrast Light Black text on white backgrounds (WCAG-style) Grayscale Remove colors from page Highlight Links Make links stand out clearly Dyslexia Friendly Use easier-to-read font Text Spacing Increase line & letter spacing Reading Guide Highlight current line while reading Large Cursor Increase cursor size Hide Images Hide all images on the page Core Controls Reset 100% Text Size 100% Line Height 1.5 Letter Spacing 0px Reset All"
